MAC Alias Cache

OPNsense resolves MAC-type firewall aliases through a cache that keeps
the last addresses of a MAC which host discovery no longer reports for
up to 12 hours. This plugin shows what each MAC alias resolves to and
rebuilds every MAC alias, and every alias nesting one, from current
host discovery data on demand (Firewall > Diagnostics > MAC Alias Cache,
or "configctl macaliascache flush").

It can also give each MAC alias the IPv6 addresses host discovery
misses. Host discovery records an address when it sees a host send a
neighbor solicitation or advertisement from it. A host that answers for
a privacy address from its stable address never gets a row for the
privacy address (opnsense/hostwatch#39), and a rule on the alias then
misses the connections the host opens from it. With the setting "Add
addresses from the neighbor table" on the plugin's page turned on, and
while host discovery is on, the plugin adds to core's cache, each
minute, the IPv6 addresses the kernel's neighbor table has for a MAC of
a MAC alias and host discovery does not list for that MAC; core's own
alias update puts them in the alias. The setting is off by default, and
turning it off takes the addresses out again.

Limits of the addresses from the neighbor table:

* IPv6 only, and only while host discovery is on (with it off, core
  reads the neighbor table itself). The aliases never hold more than 32
  addresses for one MAC and 1024 in all from it: over a limit the
  addresses now in the neighbor table come first, and the page counts
  the ones left out
* The neighbor table is read up to 1 MiB of ndp's output, about 6,000
  entries. A larger one counts as unreadable: nothing is added, and the
  addresses already added expire after 12 hours; the page says so once
  it has lasted ten minutes
* Up to about 90 seconds pass between the firewall first resolving a
  new address and the alias holding it: the plugin reads the neighbor
  table at second 30 of each minute, core rebuilds the aliases at
  second 0. Three minutes in the rare minute core skips an alias it has
  just rebuilt. A connection opened earlier keeps the rule it matched
* After the firewall itself restarts, core's cache and the neighbor
  table start empty: each of these addresses is out of its alias until
  its host talks again and both runs have passed, and the connections
  hosts reopen in those first minutes keep the rule they matched
* An address stays 12 hours after it left the neighbor table, the
  lifetime of core's cache; a flush drops it at once
* A MAC alias with an update frequency is rebuilt at that frequency,
  not each minute, so it gains and loses these addresses only then
* An address host discovery lists and later drops while its neighbor
  entry lives is out of the alias for about a minute, until the plugin
  has added it and core has applied it

Plugin Changelog
================

1.4

* A new setting, off by default, so an upgrade changes no alias until
  it is turned on: Firewall > Diagnostics > MAC Alias Cache, "Add
  addresses from the neighbor table", then Apply
* With it on, and while host discovery is on, every MAC alias also holds
  the IPv6 addresses the kernel's neighbor table has for its MACs and
  host discovery does not list. A job of the package's own
  (/usr/local/etc/cron.d/macaliascache, each minute at second 30) adds
  them to core's MAC alias cache under keys of its own, and core's own
  alias update applies them; a minute with nothing to change takes no
  lock and writes nothing. Never more than 32 addresses for one MAC and
  1024 in all
* Turned off again, the plugin's keys leave the cache at once and their
  addresses leave the aliases at core's next run, also when core has
  changed what the adding relies on. Removing the package does the same
* With the setting off and none of its keys in core's cache, the minute
  job does nothing at all: it reads no file of core's, runs no program
  and takes no lock
* A flush keeps these addresses, as the neighbor table is current data
  too; with the setting off it drops them like every stale entry
* Status and the page list the addresses that came from the neighbor
  table under their MAC. While the setting is on the page warns when
  the update has failed for ten minutes, each cause in plain words,
  when core no longer merges such keys, when a limit kept addresses out
  and when no update has completed for two hours
* A third configd action, neighbors, runs one such update; like the
  others it is served to wheel and wwwonly alone
* The note for core's fix of core #10114 no longer says the plugin
  would be unnecessary

1.3

* Both configd actions name allowed_groups (wheel, wwwonly): a local
  account in neither group can neither flush nor read the MAC and
  address list through status
* A flush reads core's reply. The page warns when core reports an error
  loading a table, and the reply carries core's status and messages
* A table core's update left empty is flushed only after an update that
  ran to its end and answered. After one that crashed, was killed or
  printed no reply, such a table keeps its addresses, and the page says
  so
* A MAC alias whose lifetime in core's alias table file is not above 0
  (an update frequency that comes to less than a second) can never be
  rebuilt by a flush; status and the page name it. The lifetime is read
  as core reads it, fractions included
* The page shows the texts of core and of the plugin as written, without
  HTML entities

1.2_1

* The page shows core's fix for core #10114 as an information note, since
  it may make the plugin unnecessary, and no longer shows core's fix for
  core #10950, which leaves nothing to do; status lists both

1.2

* The core-contract check also covers the host list's columns and flag
  that core's cache reads, and the expiry a flush relies on; a flush
  refuses when core changed any of them
* Core's fix for core #10950 shows as an information note instead of a
  warning, and the plugin now also notes core's fix for core #10114;
  neither blocks a flush
* The host list's source shows as a line on the page; host discovery
  being off is no longer a warning
* The page compares cached and current addresses in canonical form

1.1_2

* The source moved to github.com/LegoTypes/plugins; no change to
  the plugin.

1.1_1

* A flush keeps core's MAC alias cache at core's own mode (0640) instead of
  making it world-readable, and narrows one an earlier flush widened

1.1

* State and lock files move out of /tmp into a root-owned directory; files are
  checked for type, owner and shape when read back
* Flush refuses read-only users and runs one at a time
* Host rows are validated (MAC and IP shape) before they reach the alias cache;
  the core files the plugin relies on are checked, and a flush refuses to run
  when they no longer match

1.0

* Initial release
